openstack-keystone — OpenStack Identity (Keystone) 30.0.0 (2026.2 Hibiscus)
==========================================================================

This package installs the Keystone identity service. It installs software
ONLY: nothing is enabled, started, migrated or credentialed at install
time.

What it ships
-------------

  /usr/bin/keystone-manage          administrative CLI (db_sync, bootstrap, ...)
  /usr/bin/keystone-status          status CLI
  keystone.wsgi.api:application     the supported WSGI application
  keystone/common/sql/migrations/   database migration scripts
  /etc/keystone/logging.conf.sample
  /etc/keystone/sso_callback_template.html
  /usr/share/keystone/keystone.conf config-generator input
  /etc/init.d/keystone              OpenRC service (gunicorn)
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/keystone.service   systemd service (gunicorn)
  /etc/conf.d/keystone              WSGI container runtime settings

Operator steps (NOT performed by this package)
----------------------------------------------

1. Generate and edit the configuration:

     oslo-config-generator \
       --config-file /usr/share/keystone/keystone.conf \
       --output-file /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
     # edit /etc/keystone/keystone.conf (database, tokens, federation, ...)

2. Initialise the database (against a MariaDB/PostgreSQL the operator
   provides; no database server is installed by this package):

     keystone-manage db_sync

3. Bootstrap the admin identity and endpoints (operator-chosen secrets):

     keystone-manage bootstrap ...

4. Enable and start the service explicitly:

     rc-update add keystone default && rc-service keystone start
     # or
     systemctl enable --now keystone

Keystone is served by gunicorn; nginx/haproxy fronting it for TLS is an
optional, operator-configured choice.

Independence
------------

Keystone depends only on the native python closure, gunicorn and a
database client. It does not depend on QEMU, Nova, Neutron or any other
OpenStack service, and it can be installed on its own.
